1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,339
Here's the question
this tutorial asks a language model:

2
00:00:03,339 --> 00:00:04,949
how is Tolminc cheese made?

3
00:00:05,269 --> 00:00:07,504
Tolminc is a real Alpine cheese,

4
00:00:07,504 --> 00:00:11,136
but the answer we want lives
in one particular text.

5
00:00:11,383 --> 00:00:16,594
The knowledge base is eleven books:
ten old cheese books from Project Gutenberg,

6
00:00:16,594 --> 00:00:19,916
and the instructor's short story,
a fictional tale.

7
00:00:20,236 --> 00:00:23,899
A model trained before that story
existed has never read it,

8
00:00:23,899 --> 00:00:25,975
so it answers from somewhere else.

9
00:00:26,295 --> 00:00:29,730
How do we make it answer from this story,
without retraining it?

10
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:33,699
The lecture lists four weaknesses:
stale training data,

11
00:00:33,699 --> 00:00:37,498
no specialized knowledge,
hallucination, and no sources.

12
00:00:37,818 --> 00:00:42,063
RAG, retrieval augmented generation,
goes after the first two.

13
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:45,820
Paste all eleven books into the prompt,
and you get garbage.

14
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:49,383
Fine-tune the model, and it's slow;
that's lecture 8.

15
00:00:49,703 --> 00:00:53,753
Or let a librarian hand it
the ten most relevant pages:

16
00:00:53,753 --> 00:00:55,079
an open-book exam.

17
00:00:55,399 --> 00:00:56,423
That's RAG.

18
00:00:56,667 --> 00:00:59,427
The lecture's map of RAG
techniques is crowded,

19
00:00:59,427 --> 00:01:01,424
but it starts from four questions.

20
00:01:01,744 --> 00:01:05,563
What is an embedding,
a vector database, a query, the context?

21
00:01:05,883 --> 00:01:07,739
This video answers all four.

22
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:10,266
Open the README beside this video.

23
00:01:10,586 --> 00:01:12,998
Top left, the tracker shows its step;

24
00:01:12,998 --> 00:01:16,452
top right,
the gray tag points to the lecture slides.

25
00:01:16,772 --> 00:01:20,823
When the yellow your-turn tag appears,
pause and run the command;

26
00:01:20,823 --> 00:01:23,066
extra commands aren't in the README.

27
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:27,412
We recorded without a Google key.

28
00:01:27,732 --> 00:01:30,835
Docker,
chunking and the database ran for real;

29
00:01:30,835 --> 00:01:34,004
the embeddings
are the staff's recorded vectors.

30
00:01:34,324 --> 00:01:37,071
Where the code calls Google,
you see the command,

31
00:01:37,071 --> 00:01:39,145
the real error, and what to look for.

32
00:01:40,183 --> 00:01:42,060
Here's RAG in four boxes.

33
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:44,965
The user's question goes to the RAG code,

34
00:01:44,965 --> 00:01:47,927
which asks a vector database
for related texts.

35
00:01:48,247 --> 00:01:51,676
The code joins question
and texts into one full prompt,

36
00:01:51,676 --> 00:01:53,858
and the model's response goes back.

37
00:01:54,178 --> 00:01:56,588
The database never talks to the model.

38
00:01:56,833 --> 00:01:59,329
Open the RAG box and you get two lanes.

39
00:01:59,649 --> 00:02:01,837
On top, books are cut into chunks,

40
00:02:01,837 --> 00:02:05,055
an embedding model turns each chunk
into a vector,

41
00:02:05,055 --> 00:02:07,436
and the vectors go into the database.

42
00:02:07,756 --> 00:02:10,782
Below, the question goes
through the same model,

43
00:02:10,782 --> 00:02:13,366
and only its vector reaches the database.

44
00:02:13,616 --> 00:02:17,285
This is the pipeline slide from lecture,
with three phases.

45
00:02:17,605 --> 00:02:19,717
Indexing is the whole top lane.

46
00:02:20,037 --> 00:02:22,769
Retrieval brings the context to the prompt,

47
00:02:22,769 --> 00:02:25,755
and the model
does augmentation and generation.

48
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,730
The README calls the two lanes
step 1 and step 2,

49
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:32,622
and gives each box one flag of cli.py:

50
00:02:32,622 --> 00:02:37,264
--chunk, --embed and --load on top,
--query and --chat below.

51
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:38,503
Two rules.

52
00:02:38,823 --> 00:02:41,788
The top lane runs once, offline,
and it's slow.

53
00:02:42,108 --> 00:02:44,540
The bottom lane runs for every question.

54
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:47,862
And both lanes must use
the same embedding model,

55
00:02:47,862 --> 00:02:51,537
or the question
and the chunks don't live in the same space.

56
00:02:51,783 --> 00:02:53,234
Where does each box run?

57
00:02:53,554 --> 00:02:55,964
The chunker runs in your own container.

58
00:02:56,284 --> 00:02:59,594
Both models run at Google:
you pay for the text you send,

59
00:02:59,594 --> 00:03:02,322
with your key,
and nothing keeps running there.

60
00:03:02,642 --> 00:03:07,164
The database runs in a second container,
with its files on your laptop.

61
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:12,819
Every step is one python cli.py command,
inside that first container.

62
00:03:13,139 --> 00:03:15,080
So first, let's get it running.

63
00:03:15,317 --> 00:03:17,450
Prerequisites first: Docker.

64
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:20,100
This extra check prints its versions;

65
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,997
the script needs docker compose,
with a space.

66
00:03:23,317 --> 00:03:28,480
On a Mac your prompt looks different;
on Windows, use WSL or Git Bash.

67
00:03:28,717 --> 00:03:30,226
The README links the repo

68
00:03:30,226 --> 00:03:33,304
but doesn't print the clone command,
so here it is.

69
00:03:33,624 --> 00:03:37,880
Then, as an extra,
make a secrets folder beside the clone,

70
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:40,962
and keep the clone's name exactly llm-rag.

71
00:03:41,282 --> 00:03:43,629
Pause and do both, in your course folder.

72
00:03:45,867 --> 00:03:49,110
Now the service account,
in the Google Cloud console.

73
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:54,031
Create one called llm-service-account,
and grant it two roles:

74
00:03:54,031 --> 00:03:58,262
Storage Admin,
and Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform User.

75
00:03:58,582 --> 00:04:01,508
Open its keys tab, add a JSON key,

76
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:05,899
and save it in secrets
as llm-service-account.json.

77
00:04:06,134 --> 00:04:08,600
That key spends your project's money;

78
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:13,601
the .gitignore file has no secrets line,
so it's safe only beside the repo.

79
00:04:13,921 --> 00:04:18,212
Remember the three gates from lecture 4:
the key says who you are,

80
00:04:18,212 --> 00:04:21,984
the role what you may do,
and the API must be switched on.

81
00:04:22,234 --> 00:04:23,471
Two small slips.

82
00:04:23,791 --> 00:04:28,634
Nothing here uses Cloud Storage,
so the Agent Platform role is what matters.

83
00:04:28,954 --> 00:04:33,104
And the README never says
to enable the Vertex AI API;

84
00:04:33,104 --> 00:04:39,407
without it, the first embedding call fails
with SERVICE_DISABLED, after 5 retries.

85
00:04:39,727 --> 00:04:43,780
Pause and do it all now:
account, roles, key, and API.

86
00:04:46,017 --> 00:04:49,452
Step 1 of the next section: cd llm-rag.

87
00:04:49,772 --> 00:04:54,102
An extra look shows the scripts,
the compose file, cli.py,

88
00:04:54,102 --> 00:04:57,238
and the commit this video was recorded on.

89
00:04:57,484 --> 00:05:03,415
Step 2: in your editor, set GCP_PROJECT
on line 7 to your own Project ID,

90
00:05:03,415 --> 00:05:05,121
the ID, not the name.

91
00:05:05,441 --> 00:05:07,020
Leave line 8 alone.

92
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:10,732
Skip this, and your calls go
to the course's project and fail.

93
00:05:11,052 --> 00:05:12,225
Pause and edit.

94
00:05:15,567 --> 00:05:21,327
Lecture 4 put two containers on one network;
lecture 3 brought a key in through a mount.

95
00:05:21,647 --> 00:05:24,058
Today: both at once, with one command.

96
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:27,686
The script,
docker-shell.sh, exports three values:

97
00:05:27,686 --> 00:05:31,122
your project,
the key's path inside the container,

98
00:05:31,122 --> 00:05:32,428
and the image name.

99
00:05:32,748 --> 00:05:37,740
Then it calls docker compose run,
with --rm, for that one service.

100
00:05:37,984 --> 00:05:42,162
The compose file draws the whole picture:
one network, by name,

101
00:05:42,162 --> 00:05:43,909
and the CLI service on it,

102
00:05:43,909 --> 00:05:47,136
with an image built
from this folder if missing.

103
00:05:47,384 --> 00:05:51,736
Two mounts put the repo onto /app
and secrets onto /secrets.

104
00:05:52,056 --> 00:05:56,130
Two slots take the script's values,
and the service depends on Chroma.

105
00:05:56,367 --> 00:06:00,890
The Chroma service runs the database
from Docker Hub, under a fixed name.

106
00:06:01,210 --> 00:06:03,730
It publishes port 8000 to your laptop,

107
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:06,752
keeps its data
in the docker-volumes/ folder,

108
00:06:06,752 --> 00:06:09,103
and lets the course's web tools in.

109
00:06:09,350 --> 00:06:13,983
From the Dockerfile:
the virtual environment lives in /.venv,

110
00:06:13,983 --> 00:06:17,478
outside /app,
so the code mount can't hide it.

111
00:06:17,798 --> 00:06:21,852
Edit cli.py on your laptop and rerun;
no rebuild.

112
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,895
Step 3: sh docker-shell.sh.

113
00:06:25,215 --> 00:06:28,403
Compose pulls Chroma's image,
creates the network,

114
00:06:28,403 --> 00:06:30,890
and creates the Chroma container first.

115
00:06:31,134 --> 00:06:33,865
Then this,
which looks like an error but isn't.

116
00:06:34,185 --> 00:06:39,792
Compose first asks Docker Hub
for an image called llm-rag-cli, finds none,

117
00:06:39,792 --> 00:06:41,459
and builds it instead.

118
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:43,705
The build took 81 seconds here.

119
00:06:44,025 --> 00:06:46,328
Its context was 112 megabytes,

120
00:06:46,328 --> 00:06:51,855
mostly git history that still holds
an old copy of the staff's database.

121
00:06:52,175 --> 00:06:55,268
With no .dockerignore file,
the image keeps it.

122
00:06:55,517 --> 00:06:57,252
Then the banner:

123
00:06:57,252 --> 00:07:03,218
Python 3.12.15 and uv 0.13.1,
at the time of recording.

124
00:07:03,538 --> 00:07:06,888
It says activated before it activates:
cosmetic.

125
00:07:07,134 --> 00:07:08,862
You're at the container's prompt.

126
00:07:09,182 --> 00:07:12,631
The part after the at sign
is the container's ID;

127
00:07:12,631 --> 00:07:14,110
it changes every run.

128
00:07:14,430 --> 00:07:16,179
Pause, and get to this prompt.

129
00:07:19,517 --> 00:07:21,821
Check the picture with extras:

130
00:07:21,821 --> 00:07:25,661
open a second laptop terminal
and cd into llm-rag.

131
00:07:25,981 --> 00:07:30,034
The docker ps command shows Chroma
with port 8000 published.

132
00:07:30,354 --> 00:07:34,156
But the CLI container
isn't called llm-rag-cli:

133
00:07:34,156 --> 00:07:36,584
compose run ignores that name.

134
00:07:36,817 --> 00:07:38,008
And docker inspect,

135
00:07:38,008 --> 00:07:40,956
with the name from docker ps,
lists the mounts.

136
00:07:41,276 --> 00:07:44,531
The repo is on /app, secrets on /secrets,

137
00:07:44,531 --> 00:07:48,422
and the docker-volumes/
folder on Chroma's /data.

138
00:07:48,784 --> 00:07:53,008
Inside the container,
your Project ID and the key's path arrived.

139
00:07:53,328 --> 00:07:58,682
Our secrets folder is empty, with no key;
yours should list the JSON file.

140
00:07:58,917 --> 00:08:02,693
The CLI finds Chroma by name,
through the network's phone book.

141
00:08:03,013 --> 00:08:07,040
From your laptop,
the heartbeat comes through localhost:8000,

142
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:09,946
the published door for you and your browser.

143
00:08:10,184 --> 00:08:13,984
Docker already created
Chroma's data folder on your laptop,

144
00:08:13,984 --> 00:08:16,819
owned by root, with the database file in it.

145
00:08:17,139 --> 00:08:19,954
On Linux, deleting it later will need sudo.

146
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:22,248
Now two traps, hit on purpose.

147
00:08:22,568 --> 00:08:25,384
Trap one:
typing the compose command yourself.

148
00:08:25,704 --> 00:08:30,365
Both variables come out blank,
and even chunking dies at startup,

149
00:08:30,365 --> 00:08:35,816
where cli.py creates its Google client,
looking for a key file with no name.

150
00:08:36,136 --> 00:08:37,886
Always start through the script.

151
00:08:38,134 --> 00:08:42,827
Trap two: a clone with another name,
like the folder a zip download gives you.

152
00:08:43,147 --> 00:08:46,403
The compose file mounts
a folder named llm-rag,

153
00:08:46,403 --> 00:08:50,699
so Docker creates an empty one,
owned by root, over your code.

154
00:08:51,019 --> 00:08:53,600
Keep the name, and delete the stray folder.

155
00:08:53,850 --> 00:08:56,823
One last oddity:
the image tag called latest,

156
00:08:56,823 --> 00:09:01,317
and three version numbers that don't match;
a team should pin a tag.

157
00:09:01,637 --> 00:09:05,925
Pause and try these checks;
your key in /secrets matters most.

158
00:09:09,267 --> 00:09:11,038
Indexing starts with chunking.

159
00:09:11,358 --> 00:09:14,372
One vector for a whole book
averages its topics,

160
00:09:14,372 --> 00:09:16,947
so every question fetches the whole book.

161
00:09:17,267 --> 00:09:21,619
Sending 11 books each time is slow, costly,
and gives garbage.

162
00:09:21,939 --> 00:09:24,712
A chunk is small enough to find,
and to paste.

163
00:09:26,550 --> 00:09:29,857
Step 1 is a link:
the README's chunk visualizer.

164
00:09:30,177 --> 00:09:34,508
Open it in Chrome and play with the sliders;
here's the idea it shows.

165
00:09:35,350 --> 00:09:37,249
Character splitting is a ruler.

166
00:09:37,569 --> 00:09:43,371
Lines 185 and 186 set
windows of 350 characters,

167
00:09:43,371 --> 00:09:45,547
overlapping by 20.

168
00:09:45,867 --> 00:09:49,638
The separator is empty,
so it counts characters,

169
00:09:49,638 --> 00:09:52,310
not words, stepping 330 at a time.

170
00:09:52,617 --> 00:09:56,841
The instructor's story
is 7,808 characters long.

171
00:09:57,161 --> 00:10:01,769
Windows of 350 that overlap by 20:
how many chunks?

172
00:10:10,617 --> 00:10:16,974
The first window takes 350 characters,
and each one after it adds 330 new ones.

173
00:10:17,294 --> 00:10:23,652
7,458 more characters need 23 more windows:
24 chunks.

174
00:10:24,717 --> 00:10:29,986
Step 2: python cli.py --chunk
--chunk_type char-split.

175
00:10:30,306 --> 00:10:34,001
It reads 11 books
and prints each book's chunk count;

176
00:10:34,001 --> 00:10:35,256
the story gets 24.

177
00:10:35,576 --> 00:10:38,584
No key needed:
this branch never calls Google.

178
00:10:38,904 --> 00:10:40,056
Pause and run it.

179
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:49,575
An extra line count of the chunk files:
24 for the story, and 8,248 in total.

180
00:10:49,817 --> 00:10:51,438
An extra look at the seam.

181
00:10:51,758 --> 00:10:54,873
Chunk 0 stops two letters
into the word accomplish.

182
00:10:55,193 --> 00:10:57,199
Chunk 1 backs up 20 characters,

183
00:10:57,199 --> 00:11:01,017
to the last letter of things,
so accomplish is whole again.

184
00:11:01,337 --> 00:11:04,580
The lecture's pieces sit end to end,
with no overlap.

185
00:11:04,817 --> 00:11:09,468
The chunk files land in outputs/,
on your laptop, and git ignores them.

186
00:11:09,788 --> 00:11:13,728
Row 0 of a Gutenberg book
is the Project Gutenberg header:

187
00:11:13,728 --> 00:11:15,859
these books start with a header

188
00:11:15,859 --> 00:11:19,366
and end with a license,
both chunked like the rest.

189
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:25,972
Recursive splitting gets only a chunk size,
the same 350.

190
00:11:26,292 --> 00:11:27,849
No overlap is given.

191
00:11:28,683 --> 00:11:31,350
It works like scissors
that look for the best gap.

192
00:11:31,670 --> 00:11:35,401
First a blank line between paragraphs,
then a line break,

193
00:11:35,401 --> 00:11:39,328
then a space, and only as a last resort,
a single character.

194
00:11:39,648 --> 00:11:41,697
So every cut falls between words.

195
00:11:42,333 --> 00:11:45,981
Step 3: the same command
with --chunk_type recursive-split.

196
00:11:46,301 --> 00:11:49,173
The story now makes 35 chunks,

197
00:11:49,173 --> 00:11:53,576
and an extra line count
gives 14,228 in total.

198
00:11:53,896 --> 00:11:55,048
Pause and run it.

199
00:11:57,283 --> 00:11:59,886
Same chunk size, 350.

200
00:12:00,206 --> 00:12:06,243
So why 14,228 chunks instead of 8,248?

201
00:12:18,033 --> 00:12:19,505
Look at the first chunks.

202
00:12:19,825 --> 00:12:24,134
Chunk 1 starts again with Chapter One,
repeating the end of chunk 0.

203
00:12:24,454 --> 00:12:29,104
The splitter's library default
is an overlap of up to 200 characters,

204
00:12:29,104 --> 00:12:31,260
which the README never mentions.

205
00:12:31,580 --> 00:12:34,225
Cutting at boundaries
also makes chunks shorter.

206
00:12:34,467 --> 00:12:37,838
Overlap is insurance
against cutting an idea in half.

207
00:12:38,158 --> 00:12:41,955
But every repeated character is embedded,
stored, and paid for.

208
00:12:42,275 --> 00:12:45,624
If you want a different value,
set chunk_overlap yourself.

209
00:12:45,867 --> 00:12:50,368
The lecture's point is that ideal chunks
follow the document's own structure.

210
00:12:50,688 --> 00:12:54,123
Recursive splitting goes part of the way,
using paragraphs.

211
00:12:54,443 --> 00:12:57,237
Semantic chunking, in part 2, goes further.

212
00:12:59,183 --> 00:13:00,847
Next, the embedding model.

213
00:13:01,167 --> 00:13:04,940
It turns each chunk into one vector,
a list of numbers,

214
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:08,232
so similar meanings point
in similar directions:

215
00:13:08,232 --> 00:13:12,004
in this sketch, curd near curd,
license text elsewhere.

216
00:13:12,550 --> 00:13:15,900
The personality game
from lecture makes this concrete:

217
00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:19,249
which of three candidates
is most like the instructor?

218
00:13:19,569 --> 00:13:22,854
Five scores make each person
a five-number vector.

219
00:13:23,174 --> 00:13:27,888
Cosine similarity compares only the angle,
and picks the first candidate.

220
00:13:28,133 --> 00:13:30,254
The dot product also counts length,

221
00:13:30,254 --> 00:13:33,829
and would pick the second candidate,
whose arrow is longer.

222
00:13:34,149 --> 00:13:38,330
So a measure is a choice;
for text, cosine is the usual one.

223
00:13:38,866 --> 00:13:45,739
The tutorial's model, gemini-embedding-001,
natively returns 3,072 numbers;

224
00:13:45,739 --> 00:13:48,707
the code asks for the first 256,

225
00:13:48,707 --> 00:13:53,437
a prefix the model is trained
to make useful alone.

226
00:13:53,683 --> 00:13:56,691
Chunks go to the model in batches of 100.

227
00:13:57,011 --> 00:14:02,643
On an API error, it retries up to 5 times,
doubling the wait from 5 seconds.

228
00:14:02,883 --> 00:14:05,763
Your question will use
this same model, later.

229
00:14:06,083 --> 00:14:08,771
Chunks now, questions later: one model.

230
00:14:09,016 --> 00:14:13,916
Step 1: python cli.py --embed
--chunk_type char-split:

231
00:14:13,916 --> 00:14:16,184
the first call to Google.

232
00:14:16,504 --> 00:14:20,899
On our machine it reads the first book,
then stops at the missing key file.

233
00:14:21,219 --> 00:14:22,648
On yours, it runs.

234
00:14:22,883 --> 00:14:25,520
With your key, look for each book's file,

235
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:28,801
a 5-second pause,
a table with an embedding column,

236
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:31,566
and new embeddings files beside the chunks.

237
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:34,957
If yours fails,
or keeps retrying, read the error.

238
00:14:35,277 --> 00:14:38,413
Key file not found:
check its name in secrets.

239
00:14:38,733 --> 00:14:42,105
PERMISSION_DENIED:
fix the Project ID or the role,

240
00:14:42,105 --> 00:14:44,600
then exit and start the script again.

241
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:47,800
SERVICE_DISABLED: switch on the API.

242
00:14:48,050 --> 00:14:50,738
Step 2 does the same for recursive-split.

243
00:14:51,058 --> 00:14:53,900
Pause and run both, with your key:

244
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:58,247
about 89 and 148 calls,
a few cents by our estimate.

245
00:14:58,567 --> 00:15:00,146
Check the current price list.

246
00:15:02,383 --> 00:15:05,308
The video goes
on with the staff's own outputs,

247
00:15:05,308 --> 00:15:08,420
once committed and still
in every clone's history.

248
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:11,242
Their chunk files match ours byte for byte,

249
00:15:11,242 --> 00:15:13,860
so their vectors
are what this command makes.

250
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:16,425
An extra check on the story's vectors.

251
00:15:16,745 --> 00:15:19,881
24 rows of 256 numbers.

252
00:15:20,201 --> 00:15:26,089
Their lengths run
from 0.369 to 0.402, not 1.

253
00:15:26,333 --> 00:15:29,725
When we load them,
the database will compare by cosine,

254
00:15:29,725 --> 00:15:31,944
and these arrows are shorter than 1.

255
00:15:32,264 --> 00:15:34,632
Does that change
which chunks count as nearest?

256
00:15:43,466 --> 00:15:45,834
No: cosine only sees the angle.

257
00:15:46,154 --> 00:15:50,100
With straight-line distance,
Chroma's default, or a dot product,

258
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:53,429
length would leak into the ranking:
the game's lesson.

259
00:15:56,267 --> 00:15:59,462
An ordinary database
answers precise questions,

260
00:15:59,462 --> 00:16:01,569
like a price between 10 and 50,

261
00:16:01,569 --> 00:16:05,376
but it can't find rows
whose meaning is like a question.

262
00:16:05,696 --> 00:16:07,296
A vector database can.

263
00:16:07,534 --> 00:16:10,483
Each chunk becomes a record:
an ID, the text,

264
00:16:10,483 --> 00:16:13,891
metadata with book, author and year,
and the vector.

265
00:16:14,211 --> 00:16:18,115
The ID hashes the book's name plus
the row number, so it's stable.

266
00:16:18,350 --> 00:16:21,443
Records go into a collection,
one per chunk type.

267
00:16:21,763 --> 00:16:24,831
The load step deletes it
and creates it again,

268
00:16:24,831 --> 00:16:28,633
with cosine as its measure:
every load is a full rebuild.

269
00:16:28,867 --> 00:16:32,152
Comparing a question
with every vector is slow at scale.

270
00:16:32,472 --> 00:16:36,486
So the database builds an index,
in these slides' sense:

271
00:16:36,486 --> 00:16:41,432
a layered graph called HNSW,
where a search hops down in a few steps.

272
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:46,915
Step 1: python cli.py --load
--chunk_type char-split.

273
00:16:47,235 --> 00:16:50,275
On our first run there
were no embeddings yet:

274
00:16:50,275 --> 00:16:53,315
it made an empty collection
and found 0 files.

275
00:16:53,635 --> 00:16:56,494
If you see 0, your embed step didn't finish.

276
00:16:56,734 --> 00:16:58,854
On the staff's recorded vectors,

277
00:16:58,854 --> 00:17:01,903
the same command deletes
that empty collection

278
00:17:01,903 --> 00:17:04,819
and reads 11 files with an embedding column.

279
00:17:05,139 --> 00:17:10,302
It inserts 8,248 records, in batches of 500.

280
00:17:10,550 --> 00:17:15,691
Step 2 loads recursive-split,
14,228 records.

281
00:17:16,011 --> 00:17:17,718
Pause and run both loads.

282
00:17:19,967 --> 00:17:21,196
An extra check:

283
00:17:21,196 --> 00:17:26,687
both collections now have dimension 256,
fixed by the first insert.

284
00:17:26,934 --> 00:17:29,227
And one book has no author or year:

285
00:17:29,227 --> 00:17:32,438
a small slip cuts its file name
at the first dot.

286
00:17:32,758 --> 00:17:37,680
Split off only the extension,
delete that book's old files in outputs/,

287
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:39,968
then chunk, embed and load again.

288
00:17:40,217 --> 00:17:45,892
The docker-volumes/ folder on your laptop
now holds a 60-megabyte SQLite file

289
00:17:45,892 --> 00:17:50,094
and two index folders,
one per collection, owned by root.

290
00:17:50,414 --> 00:17:52,292
The README calls it persistent.

291
00:17:52,534 --> 00:17:55,606
Step 3 is a link to the course's Chroma UI.

292
00:17:55,926 --> 00:17:57,089
Opened in Chrome,

293
00:17:57,089 --> 00:18:00,304
it reads your local database
through port 8000;

294
00:18:00,304 --> 00:18:03,200
Chroma's settings let the course's site in.

295
00:18:03,450 --> 00:18:06,074
The top lane is done: the index is built.

296
00:18:06,394 --> 00:18:08,549
In part 2, we ask it questions.

